ot be 2).\n- If n=3: allowed o ∈ {0,2} (but n cannot be 3).\n\nThus possible (n,o) combos:\n\n- n=0, o=2\n- n=1, o=2\n- n=1, o=3\n\nc15: allowed (o,p): [ [2,3], [3,3], [0,2], [1,2], [1,1], [3,2] ]. So for each o:\n\n- o=2: allowed p ∈ {3,2,1}? Actually check: (2,3) gives p=3; (3,2) is (o=3,p=2) not relevant; (0,2) is (o=0,p=2) not; (1,2) is (o=1,p=2) not; (1,1) is (o=1,p=1) not; (3,3) is (o=3,p=3) not. So for o=2, only (2,3) is allowed => p=3.\n\nThus if o=2, p=3.\n\n- o=3: allowed p ∈ {3,2} (from (3,3) and (3,2)). So p ∈ {3,2}.\n\n- o=0: allowed p ∈ {2} (from (0,2)). So p=2.\n\n- o=1: allowed p ∈ {2,1} (from (1,2) and (1,1)). So p ∈ {2,1}.\n\nThus for our possible (n,o) combos:\n\nCase 1: n=0, o=2 → p=3.\n\nCase 2: n=1, o=2 → p=3.\n\nCase 3: n=1, o=3 → p ∈ {3,2}.\n\nNow c16: allowed (p,m): [ [1,0], [1,1], [0,1], [3,2], [3,3], [0,0] ]. Since m=1, allowed p values: (p,m) = (p,1) must be allowed. Which pairs have second component 1? (1,1) is allowed (p=1,m=1). Also (0,1) is allowed (p=0,m=1). Also (1,0) is (p=1,m=0) not relevant. So p ∈ {0,1}.\n\nThus p must be 0 or 1.\n\nNow check our possible p values:\n\nCase 1: p=3 → not allowed (p must be 0 or 1). So invalid.\n\nCase 2: p=3 → invalid.\n\nCase 3: p ∈ {3,2} → both invalid (p must be 0 or 1). So no solution for component c4 with m=1.\n\nThus the CSP appears unsatisfiable given our current deductions. However, maybe we made a mistake in interpreting c16 allowed pairs. Let's double-check c16: allowed (p,m): [ [1,0], [1,1], [0,1], [3,2], [3,3], [0,0] ]. So (p,m) pairs:\n\n- (1,0)\n- (1,1)\n- (0,1)\n- (3,2)\n- (3,3)\n- (0,0)\n\nThus for m=1, allowed p values are those where second component is 1: (1,1) and (0,1). So p ∈ {0,1}. So correct.\n\nThus component c4 appears unsatisfiable.\n\nThus perhaps we made an error earlier: maybe c17 allowed (b,e) is [ [1,3], [3,3] ] which forces e=3, but maybe there are other possibilities? Let's double-check c17: allowed (b,e): [ [1,3], [3,3] ]. So indeed e must be 3 (both pairs have e